#### Failure Taxonomy

See the full retry/escalation table in [§10 — Failure Escalation Flow](#failure-escalation-flow). Error classes are namespaced `transient.*` (retryable) and `terminal.*` (not retryable, escalate immediately).

**Capability Request** (Law 2 — capabilities are requested, never owned):
```
CapabilityRequest {
  requester_id, space_id,
  capability,                  // e.g. "browser.navigate"
  params, timeout, budget,
  idempotency_key              // a retry with the same key MUST return the original result, never re-execute
}
CapabilityResponse {
  status: ok | failed | denied,
  result | error, cost
}
```

**SecretRef** (secret values remain inside the execution sandbox):
```
SecretRef {
  uri: secret://<provider>/<name>
}
```
`CapabilityRequest.params` may contain `SecretRef` URIs, never raw secret values. The Sandbox Manager resolves a `SecretRef` inside the execution sandbox at the last possible moment. The Bus rejects any Pulse payload containing a resolved secret, and the schema validator enforces that raw secret values are structurally invalid in every Pulse field.

✅ **Done =** a DB password passed in `params` never appears in the Event Timeline, any Handoff Note, any compaction summary, or any `task.failed` message — verified by a validator rule, not by convention.

**Tool and Skill Registration** (all capability providers):
```
ToolRegistration {
  tool_id, version, content_hash, signature, capability,
  risk_tier: low | high,        // set at registration; binds to content_hash and is immutable except through human-approved security.grant.approved
  registered_by
}
SkillRegistration {
  skill_id, version, content_hash, signature, capabilities[],
  risk_tier: low | high,        // set at registration; binds to content_hash and is immutable except through human-approved security.grant.approved
  registered_by
}
```
Spaces reference `skill_id@version`, never `@latest`; a registration with a different `content_hash` is unclassified until registered and human-approved as required.

**Task Graph & Plan Delta** (plan versioning):
```
TaskGraph {
  space_id, plan_version,      // monotonically increasing integer
  nodes[]                      // task nodes, each optionally { optional: bool } for degraded-mode skip
}
PlanDelta {
  space_id, base_version, resulting_version,
  ops[]                        // { op: add | remove | reassign | rollback, target_node_id, ... }
}
```
Plan Deltas are diffs applied against a `base_version` — never in-place mutation of a `TaskGraph`. The Space Kernel serializes Delta application as a single-writer commit (compare-and-swap on `plan_version`): if two re-plans race, the first one accepted by the Kernel wins deterministically and bumps `resulting_version`; the second is rejected with `plan.version.superseded` and must rebase. In-flight tasks whose `plan_version` is superseded are resolved per-node by the Delta's `op`: `finish` (no matching op — task completes on its original version), `checkpoint` (op targets it but work is resumable — pause and hand to the Adapter), or `cancel` (op removes it outright).

✅ **Done:** two concurrent re-plans produce a deterministic winner (whichever the Kernel's single-writer commit accepts first), and the Monitor always knows the authoritative `plan_version`.

